Threads, futexes, yield, and per-thread slots with an exit callback.

`sys::thread::make(entry, argument)` starts a thread on raw clone: one mapping holds a guard page,
its stack, a control block and its own TLS area. When entry returns the thread runs its
`thread_local` destructors and key destructors, then leaves. `join()` waits for that and unmaps;
`cleanup()` does the same and also accepts a thread already joined. Results travel through
argument. It works only in programs started by libca_runtime: in a program started by libc,
libc owns `%fs`, and make fails with `errc::not_supported`.

`sys::futex_wait(word, expected)` sleeps while a `ca::atomic<u32>` holds expected, until a
`sys::futex_wake(word, count)`; `futex_wait_until(word, expected, deadline)` gives up with
`errc::timed_out` at a monotonic deadline. Either may return early, so callers loop on the word.
Words are process-private. Linux only; elsewhere a wait yields once, which keeps every loop
correct but spinning. The primitives built on them are in `sync/`. `sys::yield()` gives the CPU
to another runnable thread.

The slots are the POSIX thread-specific keys, reduced to what C++ `thread_local` cannot do.

`sys::thread_key_create(on_exit)` makes a slot holding one pointer per thread, null in each. When
a thread other than the main one exits with a non-null value in the slot, `on_exit(value)` runs in
that thread, the slot cleared first. `sys::thread_key_set(key, value)` stores the calling thread's
value. Keys are never deleted.

There is no getter on purpose: the data itself lives in a `thread_local` variable, which is
faster to reach, and the key only arranges the cleanup. `ca::heap` uses this to hand a dying
thread's memory back, and `ca::io::out()` to flush a thread's buffered output when it ends.

```c++
#include "ca/thread.hpp"

namespace {

	struct thread_cache final {
		int items;
	};

	constinit thread_local thread_cache* cache = nullptr;

	auto release_cache(void* const value) noexcept -> void {
		static_cast<thread_cache*>(value)->items = 0;
	}

} // namespace

auto main(void) -> int {
	static thread_cache main_cache{3};
	auto key = ca::sys::thread_key_create(&release_cache);
	if (!key)
		return 1;
	cache = &main_cache;
	return ca::sys::thread_key_set(*key, cache) && cache->items == 3 ? 0 : 2;
}
```

The callback does not run for the main thread, which ends with the process; flush or release
main-thread state from `main` or an atexit handler instead.
